+## Model Build-and-Verify Checklist (MANDATORY for multi-sheet economic/financial models)
+When a task involves a template workbook with multiple sheets (e.g., macro/production-function models), complete **all** steps below before delivery.
+
+### 1) Populate required source tables/ranges (coverage checks)
+- Fill every required source sheet/range called out by the instructions/template (e.g., PWT tables, WEO_Data, CFC data).
+- Confirm non-empty coverage across the expected rows/years/countries (spot-check multiple rows near the top/middle/bottom of the expected range).
+- Do not leave required columns blank just because downstream formulas exist.
+
+### 2) Implement projections/extensions using formulas (never pasted values)
+- Any extension/projection (e.g., years beyond last observed year) MUST be done with formulas that can be filled down/right.
+- Ensure required Excel function families are present where instructed/expected:
+  - **LN** for log transforms (e.g., LnK/LnY)
+  - **EXP** for back-transforming log trends into levels (e.g., Y* calculations)
+  - **TREND** for extending trends (e.g., log TFP trend beyond sample)
+  - **AVERAGE** for anchors/assumptions (e.g., average depreciation rate, average K/Y)
+  - Ratio/division formulas where model requires scaling (e.g., K/Y)
+- Sentinel requirement: if the template calls for a specific key formula cell (e.g., depreciation average), that cell MUST contain a formula, not a blank and not a hardcoded constant.
+
+### 3) Preserve and create cross-sheet links
+- All downstream sheets must reference the correct upstream sheets (e.g., Investment -> Production, PWT/CFC/WEO_Data -> Production).
+- Use correct sheet reference syntax (`SheetName!A1`) and consistent absolute/relative references for fill operations.
+- After filling, re-check a few cells to confirm references didn’t drift (common when inserting rows/cols).
+
+### 4) Recalculate + error scan (must be clean)
+- Run recalc.py on the final workbook.
+- Fix any Excel errors anywhere in the workbook: **#REF!, #DIV/0!, #VALUE!, #NAME?, #N/A**.
+- If denominators can be zero/blank, use defensive formulas (e.g., `IFERROR`, `IF(den=0,"",num/den)`) as appropriate to keep the model error-free.
+
+### 5) Post-save persistence check (re-open verification)
+- Re-open the saved/exported workbook and confirm:
+  - Key ranges still contain formulas (not blanks)
+  - Cross-sheet links remain intact
+  - Expected projection ranges still show formulas for extended years
+  - Key sentinel cells still contain formulas
